(A man approached a philosopher and said, "Someone in the market called you a  [fool] ." The) Tj
T* (philosopher smiled and continued walking, entirely  [unbothered] . The messenger, surprised, asked,) Tj
T* ("Does it not trouble you to be  [insulted] ?" The philosopher replied, "If a stranger threw a stone that) Tj
T* (missed me entirely, would I feel  [struck] ?" The messenger shook his head. "No, because it never) Tj
T* (actually  [hit]  you." The philosopher nodded. "A word only wounds me if I choose to let it  [land] .") Tj
T* (He explained that an insult is only an opinion offered by another, and opinions carry no weight unless) Tj
T* (we agree to  [carry]  them. The messenger asked, "So you feel nothing at all?" The philosopher said, "I) Tj
T* (notice it, but I do not have to  [accept]  it." What disturbs most people is not the insult itself, but their) Tj
T* (own judgment that it must be  [true] . The philosopher added, "Ask instead: is there truth in what was) Tj
T* (said? If so, correct it. If not, why should I  [keep]  it?" The messenger began practising this himself,) Tj
T* (noticing how much lighter he felt when he stopped  [catching]  every remark aimed at him. He finally) Tj
T* (understood: we are rarely hurt by words alone. We are hurt by the meaning we choose to  [place] ) Tj
T* (upon them.) Tj
